---
/**
 * Privacy.
 *
 * Short, because there is genuinely little to say, and specific, because a
 * short privacy policy is only worth reading if it names the actual services
 * rather than saying "our trusted partners".
 *
 * Everything stated here is checkable in this repository: the analytics
 * choice is docs/DECISIONS.md D-002, the consent record is written by
 * netlify/functions/subscribe.ts, and the cart lives in localStorage because
 * of src/lib/cart.ts. If one of those changes, this page changes with it.
 */
import LegalLayout from "@components/LegalLayout.astro";
import { business, analytics, storeOpen } from "@config/site";

const TOC = [
  { id: "short", label: "The short version" },
  { id: "waitlist", label: "If you join the waiting list" },
  { id: "orders", label: "If you place an order" },
  { id: "payments", label: "Payments" },
  { id: "analytics", label: "Visitor numbers" },
  { id: "cookies", label: "Cookies and your browser" },
  { id: "who", label: "Who else handles it" },
  { id: "choices", label: "Asking for it back, or gone" },
  { id: "changes", label: "Changes to this page" },
];
---

<LegalLayout
  title="Privacy"
  description="What this site collects, which is very little: an email address if you ask to hear when ordering opens, and an order if you place one. No cookies, no advertising trackers, and no card numbers ever reach us."
  updated="2026-09-11"
  lede="This is a one person bakery selling one pastry. It collects the least it can, and this page says exactly what that is."
  toc={TOC}
>
  <h2 id="short">The short version</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>No advertising trackers and no cookies are set by this site.</li>
    <li>Your card number never reaches us. Stripe handles payment.</li>
    <li>
      An email address is collected only if you tick the box asking to hear
      when ordering opens, and it is used for that and nothing else.
    </li>
    <li>Nothing is sold, rented or shared for anybody else's marketing.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="waitlist">If you join the waiting list</h2>

  <p>
    The form stores your email address, the exact sentence you agreed to, the
    version of that sentence, the time you agreed, and the IP address the
    request came from. The IP address is there to stop one robot signing up ten
    thousand times, and for nothing else.
  </p>

  <p>
    The wording is stored with the address on purpose. If you ever ask what you
    agreed to, the answer can be produced word for word instead of paraphrased.
  </p>

  <p>
    You get one message: the day ordering opens. Asking to come off the list
    takes one email and it is done.
  </p>

  <h2 id="orders">If you place an order</h2>

  <p>
    An order needs a name, an email address to send the confirmation to, and an
    address if the tray is being delivered or shipped. For a pickup order there
    is still a billing address, because sales tax is worked out from it.
  </p>

  <p>
    Kept with the order: what you bought, the bake date you chose, the
    fulfillment method, and the cottage food disclosure you ticked along with
    the version of its wording. That record is what makes a refund or a
    question about an old order answerable.
  </p>

  {
    !storeOpen && (
      <p class="unsettled">
        <span class="unsettled-title">Nothing can be ordered yet.</span> The
        store is closed, so none of this has happened and no order data exists
        at all today.
      </p>
    )
  }

  <h2 id="payments">Payments</h2>

  <p>
    Payment is taken by Stripe on a page Stripe hosts. Your card number, expiry
    and security code go to Stripe and never touch this site or any machine of
    ours. What comes back is whether the payment worked, the last four digits,
    and the address you gave.
  </p>

  <p>
    Stripe has its own privacy policy at{" "}
    <a href="https://stripe.com/privacy" rel="noopener">stripe.com/privacy</a>,
    and as the payment processor they are the ones holding the card details.
  </p>

  <h2 id="analytics">Visitor numbers</h2>

  {
    analytics.cloudflareToken ? (
      <p>
        This site uses Cloudflare Web Analytics to count page views. It sets no
        cookies, it does not fingerprint your browser, it does not follow you
        to any other website, and it collects nothing that identifies you. It
        was chosen over Google Analytics for exactly that reason, and because
        it is about a kilobyte rather than forty five.
      </p>
    ) : (
      <p>
        No analytics script is running on this site at the moment. If one is
        switched on it will be Cloudflare Web Analytics, which sets no cookies,
        does not fingerprint your browser and collects nothing that identifies
        you. That is also why there is no cookie banner on this page.
      </p>
    )
  }

  <h2 id="cookies">Cookies and your browser</h2>

  <p>
    This site sets no cookies, which is why you have not been asked to accept
    any.
  </p>

  <p>
    Three things are stored inside your own browser rather than on a server,
    and here is all of it:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      Your cart. What is in it, the fulfillment method you picked, the ZIP you
      typed and the bake date you chose.
    </li>
    <li>
      If you joined the waiting list, the address you used and the moment you
      used it, so this browser knows not to ask you again.
    </li>
    <li>
      If you have opened the checkout page, a random reference for that
      attempt. It is created when the page loads, before you tick or type
      anything, so that coming back after a failed payment continues the same
      order rather than starting a second one. Once you tick the home kitchen
      box it also holds the version of that wording, the moment you ticked it,
      and the order reference the server gives back.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    All of it stays on your device, none of it is readable by us, and clearing
    your browser data removes it.
  </p>

  <p>
    The web host keeps ordinary server logs, including IP addresses, for a
    short period. That is how any website on the internet works and it is not
    used to build a profile of you.
  </p>

  <h2 id="who">Who else handles it</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Netlify</strong> hosts the site and runs the small functions behind ordering.</li>
    <li><strong>Stripe</strong> takes payment and holds the card details.</li>
    <li><strong>Cloudflare</strong> serves the domain and, if it is switched on, counts page views.</li>
  </ul>

  <p class="unsettled">
    <span class="unsettled-title">Not chosen yet.</span> There is no email
    sending service in place. One is needed before ordering opens, so that an
    order confirmation can reach you, and it will be named on this page before
    it is used to send anything.
  </p>

  <h2 id="choices">Asking for it back, or gone</h2>

  <p>
    Write to{" "}
    <a href={`mailto:${business.email}?subject=Privacy`}>{business.email}</a>{" "}
    and ask what is held about you, or ask for it to be deleted. Both are
    answered by {business.owner} himself.
  </p>

  <p>
    Order records are the one thing that cannot be deleted on request straight
    away: a business has to keep its sales records. The waiting list is not a
    sales record, and coming off it is immediate.
  </p>

  <p>Nothing here is sold, and there is no advertising to opt out of.</p>

  <h2 id="changes">Changes to this page</h2>

  <p>
    If what is collected changes, this page changes on the same day and the
    date at the top changes with it. If the change is significant and you are
    on the waiting list, you will be told rather than left to notice.
  </p>
</LegalLayout>
